CS 551 Programming Project 1: Custom Memory Manager

Name: Aditya Prakash
B-Number: B01157609

Project Status
Requirements are Implemented & tested also.

Implemented Overview

This project implemented a custom memory manager the efficiently manages heap memory allocation at the program level. The implementation consists of two math components:

1.	Bitmap Operations (bitmap.c)
Implemented five bitmap manipulation functions: 
•	bitmap_find_fisrt_bit(): Finds the first bit with a specified value (0 or 1) in the bitmap
•	bitmap_set_bit(): Sets a specific bit to 1
•	bitmap_clear_bit(): Clears a specific bit to 0
•	bitmap_bit_is_set(): Checks if a specific bit is set to 1
•	bitmap_print_bitmap(): Prints the bitmap (already implemented)
Key Implementation Details: 
•	Uses bitwise operations for efficient bit manipulation
•	Properly handles byte-level indexing with bit offsets
•	Includes comprehensive error checking for NULL pointers and boundary conditions
2.	Memory Manager (memory_managerc.)

Implemented four core memory management fucntions:
•	mem_mengr_init(): Initializes the memory manager with a default 8-bytes slot size list and one batch of slots
•	mem_mngr_alloc(): Allocates memory slots using a first-fit policy 
•	mem_mngr_free(): Frees allocated memory with comprehensive error detection 
•	mem_mngr_leave(): Cleans up all allocated memory before program termination 
Key Implementation Details:

Memory Allocation Strategy:
•	Pre-allocates memory in batches to reduce kernel/user space switches 
•	Uses bitmap to track free/allocated slots(1 = free, 0 = allocated)
•	Implements first-fit policy: returns the slot with the smallest address
•	Automatically expands bitmap when new batches are added
•	Dynamically allocates new batches when all current  slots are occupied 
Error Detetion:
The mem_mngr_free() function detects three type of errors:
1.	Double freeing: Attempting to free an already-freed slot
2.	Invalid pointer: Pointer outside managed memory 
3.	Misaligned pointer: Pointer not at the start of a slot
Data Structures:
•	STRU_MEM_LIST: Linked list of memory lists with different slot size
•	STRU_MEM_BATCH: Linked list of memory batches within each list
•	Bitmap: Dynamically sized bitmap tracking slot allocations status

Building the Project
(in bash): make
To build and test with the test program:
(in bash): make test
./test_main
Testing 
The implementation has been tested with:
•	Multiple allocation and deallocation cycles
•	Batch expansion scenarios 
•	All three error conditions in mem_mngr_free()
•	Different MEM_ALIGNMENT_BOUNDARY values (8, 16, 24, etc.)
•	Different MEM_BATCH_SLOT_COUNT values (8, 16, 24, etc.)

Design Decisions
1.	First-Fit Policy: Implemented using bitmap_find_first_bit() which naturally returns the first available slot
2.	Bitmap Expansion: When adding new batches, the bitmap is expanded using realloc -syle approach (allocate new, copy old, free old) to maintain old content
3.	Memory Organization: Single memory list with 8-bytes slots is used (as per projects assumption that all requests are <= 8 bytes)
4.	Error Handling: Comprehensive error checking with descriptions error message for debugging

 Files Submitted
•	bitmap.c – Bitmap operations implementation
•	memery_manager.c – Memory manager implementation 
•	memory_manager.h – Memory Manager header (unchanged)
•	common.h – Common definitions (unchanged)
•	Makefile – Build configuration 
•	README – This file

Notes
•	The implementation assumes all allocation requests are <= 8 bytes (as started in the project requirements)
•	The code is designed to work with any MEM_ALIGNMENT_BOUNDARY and MEM_BACTH_SLOT_COUNT values that are multiple of 8
•	All dynamically allocated memory is properly freed in mem_mngr_leave()
•	The implementation uses efficient bitwise operations for bitmap manipulation. 

